# ── Email ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave MAIL_MAILER=log and every notification is written to
# storage/logs/laravel.log instead of being sent.
#
# There are TWO ways to send for real, and the only difference between them is
# which password the mail server will accept.
#
# 1) GMAIL. Gmail refuses the password you sign in to Gmail with. Over SMTP it
#    only accepts a 16-character App Password — that is GOOGLE'S rule, not this
#    system's, and nothing in this project checks a password length. To get one:
#    Google account -> turn on 2-Step Verification (App passwords do not appear
#    until you do) -> Security -> App passwords -> Mail. Put the 16 characters
#    below with no spaces and no quotes.
#
#      MAIL_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
#      MAIL_USERNAME=you@gmail.com
#
# 2) YOUR OWN DOMAIN, from whoever hosts the website. Here YOU choose the
#    password, any length you like, and guests get mail from the hotel's own
#    address instead of from a Gmail account. The exact server name is in your
#    host's control panel (cPanel -> Email Accounts).
#
#      MAIL_HOST=mail.yourhotel.com
#      MAIL_USERNAME=info@yourhotel.com
#
# Some hosts want MAIL_PORT=465 with MAIL_ENCRYPTION=ssl instead of 587/tls.
# Administration -> Notification Settings has a "Check the mail server" button
# that connects and logs in WITHOUT sending anything, and tells you in one
# sentence which part the server was unhappy about.
#
# After editing this file run once:  php artisan config:clear
MAIL_MAILER=smtp
MAIL_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
MAIL_PORT=587
MAIL_ENCRYPTION=tls
MAIL_USERNAME=biltykumawat341@gmail.com
MAIL_PASSWORD=shyambilty@@3341
MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=biltykumawat341@gmail.com
